 financial advantage, and the district's Republican lean align with a Likely R consensus among nine raters; even Democratic-sponsored polls from July show him ahead by seven. Prediction markets and the polling average point to a margin near eight points, with only modest upside for Knickerbocker given national Democratic interest in Ohio.", "watch": "A late surge of national Democratic spending or fresh fall polling showing a single-digit race could move this toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 63 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Turner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Mike Turner seeks another term in a Dayton-area district that leans Republican by about four points. He has raised roughly $895,000 and holds a large cash advantage over Democrat Kristina Knickerbocker, who has raised about $363,000. A Libertarian candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nNine expert ratings cluster at Likely R, with one at Lean R and one at Solid R. The only polls, conducted in July by Democratic firms, showed Turner ahead by seven points among likely voters. Prediction markets give Knickerbocker roughly a one-in-six chance. National Democrats have advertised in Ohio, but the combination of incumbency, money, and district lean makes Turner a clear favorite, with some room for a closer race if the fall environment shifts.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1787, "margin": -5.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1387, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1203, "margin": -8.0}]}